exteria license

rights already granted under earlier licenses remain in effect.

this license covers the parts of exteria that their copyright holders have
released under it. third-party code, fonts, artwork, sounds and other
materials keep their own licenses. this license does not grant rights that
their owners have not given.

anyone may use, study, build and modify exteria for personal use.

a person who is not a collaborator may make, publish and distribute a fork
of exteria, with or without changes, for free or for a price. a fork is a
version of exteria, not a separate project that uses parts of its code.
using exteria code in a separate project requires written permission from
sqmrak and any other copyright holders whose permission is needed.

a collaborator is anyone who has contributed to exteria or has been a
member of its development team. collaborators may work on exteria and
make forks for that work. they need written permission from sqmrak before
using exteria code in a separate project, including a project owned or
published through another account. permission covers only the use agreed
to in writing and does not replace permission needed from other copyright
holders.

when you publish or distribute a fork or a build of it, you must:

- include this license and keep existing copyright and license notices
- credit every developer named in exteria's list of developers
- link to https://github.com/sqmrak/exteria
- say clearly that your version is a fork and describe your changes
- make the source code for your version available to its recipients
- keep the licenses and notices required by third-party materials

do not present a fork as the official exteria project or imply that the
exteria team endorses it without permission.

exteria is provided as is, without warranties, to the extent allowed by
law. its authors are not responsible for damages arising from its use, to
the extent allowed by law.
